1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,528
A liquid that is mostly water ends up as a
solid that is mostly fat.

2
00:00:04,528 --> 00:00:09,611
Butter is made by agitating cream until
its fat globules collide, break their

3
00:00:09,611 --> 00:00:14,957
membranes and clump together, turning a
fat-in-water emulsion into a water-in-fat

4
00:00:14,957 --> 00:00:15,221
one.

5
00:00:15,221 --> 00:00:20,211
Under the federal definition of butter
that the USDA's grade standards for butter

6
00:00:20,211 --> 00:00:25,200
rely on, the finished product must contain
at least 80 percent milkfat by weight.

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,611
That figure tells you where the process
ends.

8
00:00:27,611 --> 00:00:32,003
It does not tell you how a liquid with fat
scattered through water becomes a solid

9
00:00:32,003 --> 00:00:33,771
with water scattered through fat.

10
00:00:33,771 --> 00:00:38,454
That reversal is the real subject, and
every step of it was worked out by looking

11
00:00:38,454 --> 00:00:39,611
inside the material.

12
00:00:39,611 --> 00:00:42,005
How does butter get made from cream?

13
00:00:42,005 --> 00:00:45,754
Start with what is on screen: a
cross-section of cream.

14
00:00:45,754 --> 00:00:51,069
The background is water, carrying
dissolved milk sugar, minerals and

15
00:00:51,069 --> 00:00:56,157
Floating in it are fat globules, each one
wrapped in a thin skin called the milk fat

16
00:00:56,157 --> 00:00:57,187
globule membrane.

17
00:00:57,187 --> 00:01:01,944
That membrane is built from phospholipids
and proteins, the same broad classes of

18
00:01:01,944 --> 00:01:06,291
molecule that form the membranes of living
cells, and that is no accident.

19
00:01:06,291 --> 00:01:10,736
It is pinched off from the secreting cells
of the mammary gland as the fat leaves

20
00:01:10,736 --> 00:01:11,011
them.

21
00:01:11,011 --> 00:01:16,237
Under an ordinary light microscope, the
globules appear as separate bright spheres

22
00:01:16,237 --> 00:01:19,997
that do not merge, even when they drift
against each other.

23
00:01:19,997 --> 00:01:22,419
The membrane is what keeps them apart.

24
00:01:22,419 --> 00:01:27,291
Food scientists call this arrangement an
emulsion: droplets of one liquid held in

25
00:01:27,291 --> 00:01:30,179
another liquid they would not normally mix
with.

26
00:01:30,179 --> 00:01:32,634
Cream is an oil-in-water emulsion.

27
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:37,977
The fat is the dispersed phase, the water
is the continuous phase, and the

28
00:01:37,977 --> 00:01:41,587
continuous phase decides how the material
behaves.

29
00:01:41,587 --> 00:01:46,149
That is why cream pours, wets your finger
and rinses off in water.

30
00:01:46,149 --> 00:01:49,999
It also explains how cream is collected in
the first place.

31
00:01:49,999 --> 00:01:55,480
Fat is less dense than water, so if milk
is left standing, the globules slowly

32
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:59,628
Modern dairies speed that up with
centrifugal separators, which spin milk so

33
00:01:59,628 --> 00:02:02,248
the lighter, fat-rich fraction can be
drawn off.

34
00:02:02,248 --> 00:02:06,317
Either way, what you get is still an
emulsion, just a more crowded one.

35
00:02:06,317 --> 00:02:11,187
Here is the first tolerance that matters.
Milk fat is not one substance.

36
00:02:11,187 --> 00:02:16,414
It is a mixture of a very large number of
different triglycerides, molecules whose

37
00:02:16,414 --> 00:02:17,816
melting points differ.

38
00:02:17,816 --> 00:02:22,013
So milk fat does not melt at a single
temperature the way ice does.

39
00:02:22,013 --> 00:02:26,292
It softens across a broad range, and at
any given temperature some of it is

40
00:02:26,292 --> 00:02:28,973
crystalline solid and some of it is liquid
oil.

41
00:02:28,973 --> 00:02:34,077
On screen the contrast is simple: milk fat
is a mixture that melts gradually, not a

42
00:02:34,077 --> 00:02:36,168
single fat with one melting point.

43
00:02:36,168 --> 00:02:39,187
Butter makers depend on that in-between
state.

44
00:02:39,187 --> 00:02:44,765
If the fat inside the globules is entirely
liquid, churning tends to produce a greasy

45
00:02:44,765 --> 00:02:49,293
mess. If it is entirely solid, the
globules resist sticking together.

46
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:52,827
So how is the solid share of the fat
actually measured?

47
00:02:52,827 --> 00:02:56,424
One standard route is pulsed nuclear
magnetic resonance.

48
00:02:56,424 --> 00:03:01,246
The instrument sends radio-frequency
pulses into a sample held in a magnetic

49
00:03:01,246 --> 00:03:04,355
field and listens to the hydrogen nuclei
respond.

50
00:03:04,355 --> 00:03:09,264
Hydrogen locked in solid crystals loses
its signal very quickly; hydrogen in

51
00:03:09,264 --> 00:03:11,784
liquid oil keeps signalling for longer.

52
00:03:11,784 --> 00:03:15,853
Comparing the two parts of the signal
gives the solid fat content.

53
00:03:15,853 --> 00:03:19,953
Methods for this measurement are
standardised by the American Oil Chemists'

54
00:03:19,953 --> 00:03:23,997
Society, which is why results from
different laboratories can be compared.

55
00:03:23,997 --> 00:03:28,702
Another instrument, the differential
scanning calorimeter, measures the heat

56
00:03:28,702 --> 00:03:33,840
absorbed as fat melts, and it draws that
broad melting range as a wide, lumpy curve

57
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:35,821
rather than a single sharp peak.

58
00:03:35,821 --> 00:03:40,659
That is why cream for butter is usually
cooled and then held for hours before

59
00:03:40,659 --> 00:03:42,984
churning, a step often called ageing.

60
00:03:42,984 --> 00:03:45,714
Fat crystals take time to form and
organise.

61
00:03:45,714 --> 00:03:50,554
Churn too soon after cooling and the
crystal structure inside the globules has

62
00:03:50,554 --> 00:03:51,299
not settled.

63
00:03:51,299 --> 00:03:56,145
The exact temperatures and holding times
vary between dairies and between seasons,

64
00:03:56,145 --> 00:04:00,874
because the fat itself changes with the
cows' feed, so this script will not give

65
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:02,056
you a single number.

66
00:04:02,056 --> 00:04:07,122
What is consistent is the principle: the
cream is prepared so that its fat is

67
00:04:07,122 --> 00:04:09,688
partly solid when the agitation begins.

68
00:04:09,688 --> 00:04:14,076
The finished object is impressive, but I'm
usually more interested in the chain of

69
00:04:14,076 --> 00:04:17,501
decisions and tolerances that keeps it
from becoming a disaster.

70
00:04:17,501 --> 00:04:18,389
Now the churn.

71
00:04:18,389 --> 00:04:23,523
A common picture is that churning presses
fat out of cream like juice from fruit.

72
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:28,102
Butter is not pressed out of cream;
microscopy shows something different.

73
00:04:28,102 --> 00:04:32,619
Agitation beats air into the cream, and
the bubbles do much of the work.

74
00:04:32,619 --> 00:04:37,556
Fat globules collide with the surfaces of
those bubbles, and their membranes spread

75
00:04:37,556 --> 00:04:38,448
and thin there.

76
00:04:38,448 --> 00:04:42,955
Meanwhile, the fat crystals inside a
globule can poke through its weakened

77
00:04:42,955 --> 00:04:47,700
When two damaged globules touch, the
crystals of one can bridge into the liquid

78
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:51,965
oil of the other, and they stick without
fully merging into one sphere.

79
00:04:51,965 --> 00:04:54,303
This is called partial coalescence.

80
00:04:54,303 --> 00:04:59,847
The steps on screen run in order: air is
beaten in, membranes are damaged, crystals

81
00:04:59,847 --> 00:05:01,784
bridge globules, clumps grow.

82
00:05:01,784 --> 00:05:05,619
If you have whipped cream by hand, you
have already seen the halfway point.

83
00:05:05,619 --> 00:05:09,988
Whipped cream is a foam held up by these
partly coalesced clumps of fat, forming a

84
00:05:09,988 --> 00:05:11,640
network around the air bubbles.

85
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:16,445
That is why whipped cream holds its shape.
Keep going and the network tightens.

86
00:05:16,445 --> 00:05:21,493
The clumps grow larger, they pull fat away
from the bubble surfaces, the foam can no

87
00:05:21,493 --> 00:05:23,896
longer support itself, and it collapses.

88
00:05:23,896 --> 00:05:28,824
Microscopy of whipped cream sampled at
different moments traces this progression:

89
00:05:28,824 --> 00:05:33,752
first separate globules, then clusters
lining the bubbles, then larger aggregates

90
00:05:33,752 --> 00:05:36,368
that no longer belong to any bubble at
all.

91
00:05:36,368 --> 00:05:40,019
Butter is, in a sense, overwhipped cream
carried to its end.

92
00:05:40,019 --> 00:05:43,174
Then comes the moment butter makers call
the break.

93
00:05:43,174 --> 00:05:47,939
Quite suddenly, the smooth cream turns
into small yellow grains floating in a

94
00:05:47,939 --> 00:05:49,176
thin, watery liquid.

95
00:05:49,176 --> 00:05:54,612
The sound in the churn changes from a soft
thud to a slosh, because there is now free

96
00:05:54,612 --> 00:05:56,723
liquid moving around solid lumps.

97
00:05:56,723 --> 00:05:59,285
What has happened is a phase inversion.

98
00:05:59,285 --> 00:06:03,949
Before the break, water was the continuous
phase and fat was dispersed.

99
00:06:03,949 --> 00:06:08,875
After it, the fat grains join into a
connected mass, and the water that remains

100
00:06:08,875 --> 00:06:11,432
inside them is trapped as small droplets.

101
00:06:11,432 --> 00:06:14,861
Butter is a water-in-fat material, not a
fat-in-water one.

102
00:06:14,861 --> 00:06:19,472
That is the reversal from the cold open,
and it is why butter feels greasy and

103
00:06:19,472 --> 00:06:22,605
repels water, where cream wets everything
it touches.

104
00:06:22,605 --> 00:06:25,486
The liquid left behind is the original
buttermilk.

105
00:06:25,486 --> 00:06:30,152
It is not the thick cultured drink sold
under the same name in many supermarkets.

106
00:06:30,152 --> 00:06:35,049
It is the water phase of the cream,
carrying lactose, proteins, minerals and

107
00:06:35,049 --> 00:06:37,304
much of the torn membrane material.

108
00:06:37,304 --> 00:06:41,827
Because those membrane phospholipids are
good emulsifiers, food scientists have

109
00:06:41,827 --> 00:06:44,861
studied buttermilk as an ingredient in its
own right.

110
00:06:44,861 --> 00:06:49,215
Draining it is the first separation in the
process, and a substantial share of what

111
00:06:49,215 --> 00:06:52,835
went into the churn leaves as buttermilk
rather than becoming butter.

112
00:06:52,835 --> 00:06:56,795
The grains are still coated in buttermilk,
so they are usually washed with cold

113
00:06:56,795 --> 00:06:58,099
water, then drained again.

114
00:06:58,099 --> 00:07:03,630
Washing removes residual milk sugar and
protein that bacteria could otherwise feed

115
00:07:03,630 --> 00:07:03,832
on.

116
00:07:03,832 --> 00:07:09,049
Then comes working: kneading the mass,
traditionally with paddles, industrially

117
00:07:09,049 --> 00:07:13,011
between rollers or inside continuous
butter-making machines.

118
00:07:13,011 --> 00:07:18,032
Working presses the grains into one block
and squeezes out loose water, but its more

119
00:07:18,032 --> 00:07:22,874
important job is to break the water that
stays behind into a very large number of

120
00:07:22,874 --> 00:07:25,923
very small droplets, spread evenly through
the fat.

121
00:07:25,923 --> 00:07:30,931
If salt is added, it dissolves into those
droplets rather than into the fat.

122
00:07:30,931 --> 00:07:35,328
This internal structure is visible with
confocal laser scanning microscopy.

123
00:07:35,328 --> 00:07:40,018
Researchers stain the fat and the water
with different fluorescent dyes, and the

124
00:07:40,018 --> 00:07:44,825
instrument scans a focused laser through
the sample, building sharp optical slices

125
00:07:44,825 --> 00:07:45,997
at different depths.

126
00:07:45,997 --> 00:07:51,643
In images of well-worked butter, the water
appears as fine, scattered droplets,

127
00:07:51,643 --> 00:07:57,002
separated by a continuous fat phase that
contains crystals, surviving whole

128
00:07:57,002 --> 00:07:58,717
globules and liquid oil.

129
00:07:58,717 --> 00:08:01,695
That droplet pattern matters for keeping
quality.

130
00:08:01,695 --> 00:08:06,192
Bacteria that do enter the butter are
confined to tiny isolated pockets of

131
00:08:06,192 --> 00:08:11,053
water, many carrying little nutrient, and
in salted butter a concentrated brine.

132
00:08:11,053 --> 00:08:15,391
Poorly worked butter, with larger
connected water channels, gives microbes

133
00:08:15,391 --> 00:08:20,256
more room. This is a description of
structure, not guidance about storing

134
00:08:20,256 --> 00:08:25,084
Everything so far describes sweet cream
butter, made from cream that has not been

135
00:08:25,084 --> 00:08:25,680
fermented.

136
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:28,209
Cultured butter adds a step at the start.

137
00:08:28,209 --> 00:08:32,959
The cream is inoculated with lactic acid
bacteria and allowed to ripen before

138
00:08:32,959 --> 00:08:33,515
churning.

139
00:08:33,515 --> 00:08:38,335
The bacteria convert some of the milk
sugar into lactic acid, which sours the

140
00:08:38,335 --> 00:08:41,840
cream, and certain strains also produce
aroma compounds.

141
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:43,363
The best known is diacetyl.

142
00:08:43,363 --> 00:08:48,100
It is catalogued in PubChem, the chemical
database run by the National Institutes of

143
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:51,371
Health, where its structure and properties
can be checked.

144
00:08:51,371 --> 00:08:56,133
Flavor chemists have long linked it to the
characteristic aroma of cultured butter.

145
00:08:56,133 --> 00:09:00,971
Identifying which molecule smells of what
took a particular pairing of instruments.

146
00:09:00,971 --> 00:09:06,140
Gas chromatography separates a sample's
volatile compounds by how quickly they

147
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:08,592
travel through a long, coated column.

148
00:09:08,592 --> 00:09:13,529
In gas chromatography olfactometry, the
stream leaving the column is split: one

149
00:09:13,529 --> 00:09:18,340
part goes to a detector, the other to a
sniffing port, where a trained person

150
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:20,277
notes what they smell and when.

151
00:09:20,277 --> 00:09:25,330
Matching a buttery note to the moment a
known compound emerges is how aroma

152
00:09:25,330 --> 00:09:28,699
molecules in dairy products have been
pinned down.

153
00:09:28,699 --> 00:09:33,111
Mass spectrometry then confirms the
identity from the compound's fragmentation

154
00:09:33,111 --> 00:09:33,563
pattern.

155
00:09:33,563 --> 00:09:37,862
Research of this kind appears in journals
published by the American Chemical

156
00:09:37,862 --> 00:09:38,315
Society.

157
00:09:38,315 --> 00:09:42,448
Once butter is made, someone has to decide
whether it is any good.

158
00:09:42,448 --> 00:09:48,218
In the United States, the USDA publishes
voluntary grade standards for butter, and

159
00:09:48,218 --> 00:09:50,048
the top grade is Grade AA.

160
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:52,746
Graders assess flavor, body, color and
salt.

161
00:09:52,746 --> 00:09:57,529
Body is where the physical chemistry from
earlier returns: a grader is judging

162
00:09:57,529 --> 00:10:02,067
texture, spreadability and whether water
beads out of the cut surface, all

163
00:10:02,067 --> 00:10:05,563
consequences of crystal structure and
droplet dispersion.

164
00:10:05,563 --> 00:10:09,933
The 80 percent milkfat requirement in the
federal definition is a separate,

165
00:10:09,933 --> 00:10:11,040
compositional line.

166
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:16,435
A product can meet it and still grade
poorly, because composition says how much

167
00:10:16,435 --> 00:10:19,099
fat is present, not how it is arranged.

168
00:10:19,099 --> 00:10:21,908
Watch the cross-sections as the episode
moves.

169
00:10:21,908 --> 00:10:26,304
The cream shot shows separate spheres in a
bright continuous background.

170
00:10:26,304 --> 00:10:31,369
The butter shot inverts it: the background
is the fat, and the water has become the

171
00:10:31,369 --> 00:10:32,528
scattered droplets.

172
00:10:32,528 --> 00:10:37,797
Every transition between them is the same
few events, repeated: air, damaged

173
00:10:37,797 --> 00:10:42,581
membranes, crystals bridging globules,
clumps, and finally inversion.

174
00:10:42,581 --> 00:10:47,774
The instruments named on screen are the
reason those images are diagrams and not

175
00:10:47,774 --> 00:10:48,293
guesses.

176
00:10:48,293 --> 00:10:51,052
Here is what this evidence does not
settle.

177
00:10:51,052 --> 00:10:55,479
Microscopy shows structures in small
samples, often after staining or

178
00:10:55,479 --> 00:11:00,548
preparation that can disturb what is being
imaged, so each picture is a careful

179
00:11:00,548 --> 00:11:03,371
snapshot rather than a live film of a
churn.

180
00:11:03,371 --> 00:11:08,429
The partial coalescence account explains
the main mechanism well, but how much each

181
00:11:08,429 --> 00:11:13,427
factor contributes, from fat crystal shape
to membrane composition to churn speed,

182
00:11:13,427 --> 00:11:16,048
varies with the cream and is still
studied.

183
00:11:16,048 --> 00:11:22,134
Milk fat composition shifts with feed and
season, so no single temperature recipe is

184
00:11:22,134 --> 00:11:22,859
universal.

185
00:11:22,859 --> 00:11:27,872
And the 80 percent figure is a United
States legal definition; other countries

186
00:11:27,872 --> 00:11:31,792
set their own standards, which this
episode has not surveyed.

187
00:11:31,792 --> 00:11:35,435
Cream becomes butter when its structure is
turned inside out.

188
00:11:35,435 --> 00:11:40,152
The fat that was scattered becomes the
frame, and the water that was everywhere

189
00:11:40,152 --> 00:11:42,123
is left in small, sealed pockets.
